        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-0f2284f07f1c8448\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite directly avows that she loves Armand, making T dominant. In this moment of choosing their relationship over the duke’s support, the avowal also helps ratify her relational commitment, giving P moderate support. The murmuring, embrace, emotion, and emphatic “Oh! oui” indicate intensity but do not independently establish reflex-like expressive discharge, so E is unsupported.\",\"ambiguity\":\"P could be scored lower if the future plans and renunciation are treated as separate commitments rather than force carried by “je t’aime” itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Marguerite directly says “Oh! oui, je t’aime, mon Armand!” to Armand; the dialogue is presented within Armand’s first-person narration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite’s declaration answers Armand’s reciprocal appeal after he overhears her refuse to leave him for the duke’s financial support. Her avowal is corroborated by her stated willingness to sacrifice that support, her plans for a quiet shared life, and her subsequent changes in conduct. The scene frames the utterance as sincere, emotionally intense, and partly relationship-ratifying, while her request that Armand never reproach her past reveals vulnerability and a need for reassurance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Oh! oui, je t’aime, mon Armand! … je t’aime comme je n’aurais pas cru pouvoir aimer.”\",\"supports\":\"A direct and elaborated avowal of Marguerite’s loving emotional state strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite has just refused to leave Armand despite the duke’s threat to withdraw financial support: “je ne quitterai pas Armand … Qu’il garde son argent; je m’en passerai.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration occurs as she chooses the relationship at substantial cost, supporting sincerity and some relationship-ratifying P force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Nous serons heureux, nous vivrons tranquilles, et je dirai un éternel adieu à cette vie…”\",\"supports\":\"Immediate future-oriented undertakings strengthen the reading that the avowal participates in affirming a shared life, although these commitments are linguistically distinct from the core words.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She “murmura” the words while putting both arms around Armand’s neck; the narration also describes her voice as emotional.\",\"supports\":\"Shows intimate and affectively intense delivery, but without positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like discharge; therefore it does not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Afterward, Marguerite breaks with former friends and habits and rejects the duke’s letters without reading them.\",\"supports\":\"Subsequent conduct corroborates the avowal and its commitment-bearing context.\",\"confidence\":0.92}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the central avowal, while moderate P captures its limited relationship-ratifying force. Emotional delivery is adequately represented contextually without E, and no important residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
